.\" ============================================================================
.TH swipe 1 "September 29, 2026" "version 2.2.0" "USER COMMANDS"
.\" ============================================================================
.SH NAME
swipe \(em Smith-Waterman database searches with inter-sequence SIMD
parallelisation
.\" ============================================================================
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY swipe
.B \-h|\-\-version
.YS
.PP
.SY swipe
.B \-d
.I database
.OP \-i filename
.OP \-p type
.OP \-M matrix
.OP \-G int
.OP \-E int
.OP \-r int
.OP \-q int
.OP \-S strands
.OP \-Q code
.OP \-D code
.OP \-e real
.OP \-k real
.OP \-c int
.OP \-u int
.OP \-v int
.OP \-b int
.OP \-m format
.OP \-a int
.OP \-x filename
.OP \-z int
.OP \-o filename
.OP \-IH
.YS
.PP
.SY swipe
.B \-d
.I database
.BR \-N " 1|2"
.OP \-p type
.OP \-o filename
.OP \-H
.YS
.\" ============================================================================
.SH DESCRIPTION
SWIPE performs rapid local alignment searches of amino acid or
nucleotide query sequences against sequence databases, using the
Smith-Waterman algorithm: the optimal local alignment score is
computed for every database sequence, with no heuristic.
Speed comes from inter-sequence parallelisation: the SIMD
instructions of the processor compute the alignment scores of up to
16 database sequences at once (SSE2 is required; SSSE3 is faster).
Scores are first computed with 7-bit saturated arithmetic, and the
sequences whose score overflows are searched again with 16-bit,
then 63-bit arithmetic, so that every score is exact.
.PP
Queries are read in FASTA format, from standard input or from the
file given with
.BR \-i .
Several queries can be searched in one run: they are searched one
after the other.
Characters that are not valid sequence symbols are ignored.
.PP
The database is a BLAST database, prepared with the
.B makeblastdb
tool of NCBI BLAST+ (or with the
.B formatdb
tool of the legacy NCBI BLAST).
Databases in format version 4 and version 5 (the default of current
makeblastdb versions, and the format of NCBI's pre-formatted
databases) are accepted.
Databases split into volumes, and alias files
.RI ( .pal ,
.IR .nal )
that list several databases or select a subset of their sequences
(OIDLIST and MEMB_BIT lines), are accepted as well.
Alias files with GILIST, TAXIDLIST or SEQIDLIST lines are rejected:
the taxid list can be given with
.B \-x
instead.
.PP
The search type is chosen with
.BR \-p :
.TS
tab(@);
l l l l l.
\fB\-p\fR@name@query@database@comparisons
_
0@blastn@nucleotide@nucleotide@query strands (\fB\-S\fR)
1@blastp@amino acid@amino acid@direct (default)
2@blastx@nucleotide@amino acid@query in 6 frames
3@tblastn@amino acid@nucleotide@database in 6 frames
4@tblastx@nucleotide@nucleotide@6\(mu6 frames
5@sound@sound codes@sound codes@direct
.TE
.PP
Results are ranked by decreasing alignment score.
When statistical parameters are available for the scoring system,
E-values and bit scores are computed with the Karlin-Altschul
statistics of NCBI BLAST, from the length of the query and the
size of the database (both corrected by the length adjustment of
BLAST).
Composition-based statistics and query filtering are not
implemented.
.\" ============================================================================
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General options
.TP 9
.B \-h\fP,\fB\ \-\-help
display the version, the reference and the list of options, and
exit with status 0.
.TP
.B \-\-version
display the version and the reference, and exit with status 0.
.TP
.BI \-a\fP,\fB\ \-\-num_threads\fB\~ "positive integer"
number of threads to use, from 1 to 256 (default is 1).
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS Input options
.TP 9
.BI \-d\fP,\fB\ \-\-db\fB\~ "base name"
base name of the database (required): the name given to
makeblastdb with
.BR \-out ,
without the extension of the database files
.RI ( .pin ,
.IR .psq ,
.IR .pal ,
etc.).
.TP
.BI \-i\fP,\fB\ \-\-query\fB\~ filename
FASTA file of the queries (default is standard input, also selected
with the name \-).
.TP
.BI \-p\fP,\fB\ \-\-symtype\fB\~ "name or integer"
search type: blastn (0), blastp (1), blastx (2), tblastn (3),
tblastx (4) or sound (5); see the table above (default is blastp).
.TP
.BI \-S\fP,\fB\ \-\-strand\fB\~ "name or integer"
query strands to search, for nucleotide queries (blastn, blastx
and tblastx): plus (1), minus (2) or both (3) (default is both).
Protein queries (blastp, tblastn) cannot be searched on the minus
strand.
.TP
.BI \-Q\fP,\fB\ \-\-query_gencode\fB\~ "positive integer"
genetic code used to translate the queries of blastx and tblastx
searches (default is 1, the standard code).
Codes 1 to 6, 9 to 16 and 21 to 23 of the NCBI genetic codes are
accepted.
.TP
.BI \-D\fP,\fB\ \-\-db_gencode\fB\~ "positive integer"
genetic code used to translate the database sequences of tblastn
and tblastx searches (default is 1).
The same codes as for
.B \-Q
are accepted.
.TP
.BI \-x\fP,\fB\ \-\-taxidlist\fB\~ filename
search only the database sequences of the organisms listed in
.IR filename :
a plain text file with one NCBI taxonomy identifier (taxid) per line.
The database must contain taxids (makeblastdb
.BR \-taxid_map ).
The database size used for the E-values is not changed.
The option
.B \-\-taxid
is an alias of
.BR \-\-taxidlist .
.TP
.BI \-z\fP,\fB\ \-\-dbsize\fB\~ "positive integer or zero"
effective size of the database, in residues, used for the E-values
instead of the actual size (default is 0: the actual size).
Scientific notation is accepted (e.g. 1e9).
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS Scoring options
.TP 9
.BI \-M\fP,\fB\ \-\-matrix\fB\~ "name or filename"
score matrix of amino acid searches (default is BLOSUM62; IDENTITY_5_1
for sound searches).
The matrices BLOSUM45, BLOSUM50, BLOSUM62, BLOSUM80, BLOSUM90,
PAM30, PAM70 and PAM250 are built in (names are case-insensitive);
any other name is read as the name of a file, in the NCBI matrix
format.
Ignored in blastn searches, whose scores are set by
.B \-r
and
.BR \-q .
.TP
.BI \-r\fP,\fB\ \-\-reward\fB\~ integer
score of a nucleotide match, in blastn searches (default is 1).
.TP
.BI \-q\fP,\fB\ \-\-penalty\fB\~ integer
score of a nucleotide mismatch, in blastn searches (default is \-3).
.TP
.BI \-G\fP,\fB\ \-\-gapopen\fB\~ "positive integer or zero"
gap opening penalty.
.TP
.BI \-E\fP,\fB\ \-\-gapextend\fB\~ "positive integer or zero"
gap extension penalty: a gap of length
.I k
costs
.IR G\~+\~k\~\(mu\~E .
The two penalties cannot both be zero.
A penalty that is not given takes the default value of the score
matrix: 14+2 (BLOSUM45), 13+2 (BLOSUM50), 11+1 (BLOSUM62 and
BLOSUM80), 10+1 (BLOSUM90 and PAM70), 9+1 (PAM30) and 15+2
(PAM250); 5+2 for blastn searches, and 15+5 for sound searches.
With a matrix read from a file, at least one of the penalties must be
given, and a penalty that is not given is zero.
.PP
The file
.I scoring.pdf
of the SWIPE distribution lists the scoring systems (score matrix,
or match reward and mismatch penalty, and gap penalties) for which
statistical parameters are available.
With other scoring systems, E-values and bit scores are not
computed: hits are selected and reported by raw score only.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS Result options
.TP 9
.BI \-e\fP,\fB\ \-\-evalue\fB\~ "positive real"
maximum E-value of the hits to report (default is 10.0).
.TP
.BI \-k\fP,\fB\ \-\-minevalue\fB\~ "positive real or zero"
minimum E-value of the hits to report (default is 0.0).
.TP
.BI \-c\fP,\fB\ \-\-min_score\fB\~ "positive integer"
minimum raw alignment score (not a bit score) of the hits to report
(default is 1).
.TP
.BI \-u\fP,\fB\ \-\-max_score\fB\~ "positive integer or zero"
maximum raw alignment score of the hits to report (default: no
limit).
.TP
.BI \-v\fP,\fB\ \-\-num_descriptions\fB\~ "positive integer or zero"
number of hits described in the plain and XML outputs (default is
250).
.TP
.BI \-b\fP,\fB\ \-\-num_alignments\fB\~ "positive integer or zero"
number of hits aligned (default is 100).
The tabular outputs report one line per aligned hit: their number of
lines is set by
.BR \-b ,
not by
.BR \-v .
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS Output options
.TP 9
.BI \-m\fP,\fB\ \-\-outfmt\fB\~ integer
output format (default is 0):
.RS
.TP 4
0
plain text, similar to NCBI BLAST's pairwise output: the search
parameters, the list of hits, then their alignments;
.TP
7
simple XML: one
.I <result>
element per query, inside one
.I <results>
element;
.TP
8
tabular, one line per hit and 12 tab-separated columns, as NCBI
BLAST's
.BR "\-m 8" :
query identifier, database sequence identifier, percentage of
identity, alignment length, mismatches, gap openings, query start
and end, database sequence start and end, E-value, bit score.
Without statistical parameters, the last two columns are replaced
by the raw score;
.TP
9
tabular with comment lines: the same columns, preceded for each
query by comment lines (starting with #) naming the program, the
query, the database and the columns;
.TP
99
ParAlign XML.
.RE
.TP 9
.BI \-o\fP,\fB\ \-\-out\fB\~ filename
write the results to
.I filename
instead of standard output.
The file is created (or truncated) once all the options are checked.
.TP
.B \-I\fP,\fB\ \-\-show_gis
show the gi numbers of the database sequences, when the database
has them, in the plain and XML outputs.
The tabular outputs always show them.
.TP
.B \-H\fP,\fB\ \-\-show_taxid
show the taxids of the database sequences, and their membership and
link bits when present, after their identifiers.
.TP
.BI \-N\fP,\fB\ \-\-dump\fB\~ integer
write the database sequences in FASTA format, instead of searching:
1 writes one header line per sequence (the deflines of a sequence
of a non-redundant database are joined on that line, separated by
" >"), 2 writes one header per defline (the sequence is then
repeated after each of its deflines); 0 searches (default).
Identifiers include the gi numbers.
Sequences of translated databases (tblastn, tblastx) are written as
nucleotides.
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.SS BLAST compatibility options
These BLAST options are accepted, but only with the values that
disable the corresponding features, which SWIPE does not implement:
.TP 9
.BI \-C\fP,\fB\ \-\-comp_based_stats\fB\~ "F or 0"
composition-based statistics (F, f or 0 only).
.TP
.BI \-F\fP,\fB\ \-\-filter\fB\~ F
query filtering of low complexity regions (F, f or an empty string
only).
Pre-filter the queries if needed.
.TP
.BI \-K\fP,\fB\ \-\-subalignments\fB\~ integer
number of subalignments: accepted and ignored, SWIPE reports one
alignment per hit.
.\" ============================================================================
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 4
0
success, including when no hit is found, and for
.B \-h
and
.BR \-\-version .
.TP
1
error: an invalid option or option value, a query file or a
database that cannot be read, or an output file that cannot be
written.
A message is written to standard error.
.\" ============================================================================
.SH EXAMPLES
Search the protein queries of
.I query.fsa
against the protein database
.I swissprot
(made with makeblastdb), with 8 threads and a maximum E-value of
0.001:
.PP
.RS
.EX
swipe \-p 1 \-i query.fsa \-d swissprot \-e 0.001 \-a 8
.EE
.RE
.PP
Search a nucleotide query against the nucleotide database
.I ecoli
(both strands), with a tabular output:
.PP
.RS
.EX
swipe \-p 0 \-i primer.fsa \-d ecoli \-m 8 \-o hits.tsv
.EE
.RE
.PP
Search only the human entries of the database
.I est
(which contains taxids), with a translated search of the protein
query of
.IR prot.fsa :
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo 9606 > human.tax
swipe \-p 3 \-i prot.fsa \-d est \-x human.tax
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a database and search it with linear gap costs (no E-values,
since BLAST has no statistical parameters for these penalties):
.PP
.RS
.EX
makeblastdb \-in proteins.fasta \-dbtype prot \-out proteins
swipe \-d proteins \-i query.fsa \-G 0 \-E 2
.EE
.RE
